WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:26.451
<v Riley>Hey, welcome back. On today's episode — the Bald Range fire's damage total lands: around a hundred and fifty structures gone in our own region. Plus, Watch Duty just hit ten million users expanding into flood tracking — and we're asking why tech calls its own work meaningless while ranchers facing real fire just get a truck, a radio, and neighbours. That and more, coming right up.

00:00:42.426 --> 00:01:06.107
<v Riley>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities — I'm Riley. It's Tuesday, August 18th, 2026. Coming to you from the Cariboo, on lands that have been cared for by the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh peoples since time immemorial. Today's theme is Working Lands &amp; Industry.

00:01:07.077 --> 00:01:19.125
<v Casey>And I'm Casey. Forestry, ranching, mining, agriculture — the industries that actually pay the bills out here, and what's changing underneath them while everybody's attention is on fire season.

00:01:19.416 --> 00:01:46.006
<v Casey>Quick weather check before we get into it. Out at Horsefly Lake it's 16 degrees with overcast skies, high of 26, low of 15, partly cloudy tomorrow. Across the region — 100 Mile House at 15 and overcast, Williams Lake at 18 and overcast, Quesnel at 16 and overcast, and out on the Chilcotin plateau, Redstone's sitting at 11 with some breaks of cloud.

00:01:46.297 --> 00:01:50.036
<v Riley>Let's get into what's actually happening on the ground.

00:01:51.437 --> 00:02:07.549
<v Riley>It's a packed docket today — a heavy run of local fire and civic news out of Quesnel and Williams Lake, plus a deeper theme block than we usually get. Starting close to home: the 108 Mile Volunteer Fire Department is reactivating its air siren at the fire hall.

00:02:08.167 --> 00:02:10.527
<v Casey>The old-school alert system, back in service.

00:02:11.727 --> 00:02:33.975
<v Riley>Exactly — Cariboo Regional District communications manager Kat Chatten confirmed it'll sound between 8 a.m. and 8 p.m. whenever the department gets a call. In a fire season this active, redundancy in how you alert volunteers isn't nostalgia, it's function. Cell towers can go down, apps need data and battery — a siren just needs power and a working switch.

00:02:35.175 --> 00:03:07.975
<v Casey>Up the valley, the Nuxalk Nation has come out strongly against the draft official community plan for Bella Coola. The Williams Lake Tribune reports the Nation says the plan was drafted without adequate consultation on land use decisions that directly affect their territory — everything from zoning along the valley bottom to development near culturally significant sites. OCPs set the frame for what gets built, where, and under what conditions for the next twenty or thirty years — if a Nation says the process skipped them, that's the plan's legitimacy in question before it's even adopted.

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:14.368
<v Riley>A governance dispute that won't make national headlines but shapes decades of decisions here.

00:03:15.568 --> 00:03:25.912
<v Riley>In municipal politics, former city councillor Ron Paull has announced he's running for mayor again in Quesnel, seeking a second term when residents head to the polls this October.

00:03:27.111 --> 00:03:44.727
<v Casey>And staying in Quesnel — a structure fire this week may have been caused by someone using a torch to burn out a hornet's nest on a building. Emergency Health Services checked the property owner over, no injuries reported, and the West Fraser Fire Department got the blaze knocked down quickly with Quesnel Fire assisting.

00:03:45.337 --> 00:03:49.713
<v Riley>A hornet problem escalating into a fire call is very much a Cariboo August.

00:03:50.913 --> 00:04:05.345
<v Riley>Quesnel Fire also extinguished a structure fire at the old Maple Park Mall site on Monday. Two separate structure fire calls in the same city inside a matter of days — everything's more combustible right now, buildings included, not just forest.

00:04:06.545 --> 00:04:23.657
<v Casey>And the number that matters most today out of all of this — the Williams Lake Tribune's update puts the damage from the Bald Range wildfire at around a hundred and fifty structures destroyed or damaged. That's a significant loss for a rural area, and as of the report, it's still an active, uncontained fire.

00:04:24.926 --> 00:04:34.174
<v Riley>A hundred and fifty structures is homes, barns, equipment sheds — for a working ranching community, that's operational capacity gone, not just housing loss.

00:04:35.399 --> 00:04:41.831
<v Casey>We'll come back to what a season like this does to the people actually working that land, because it's not an abstraction here.

00:04:43.031 --> 00:05:03.743
<v Riley>One more from the province before we move on — BC Gov News has a piece on a new employment program helping people who are blind, deafblind, or have low vision build careers. Funding through a federal-provincial labour market agreement will support as many as sixteen participants with skills training, paid work experience, and job readiness support.

00:05:05.142 --> 00:05:11.422
<v Casey>Small program in absolute numbers, but sixteen people gaining real occupational skills is sixteen real outcomes.

00:05:12.621 --> 00:05:34.413
<v Casey>That Bald Range number connects directly to something much bigger happening south of the border. Big Sur's Esalen Institute — the well-known retreat and research center — was evacuated as the Timber Fire expanded past 1,700 acres, according to Business Insider's reporting. Same pattern we're watching everywhere this summer — fast-moving fire outpacing the usual notification systems.

00:05:35.584 --> 00:05:53.216
<v Riley>And Fast Company's NASA smoke-tracking piece backs that up on a continental scale — this is the fourth consecutive year that wildfire smoke from Canadian fires has caused severe air pollution as far away as New York and Detroit, tracked through a joint EPA and Forest Service mapping project.

00:05:53.732 --> 00:05:56.980
<v Casey>Four years running isn't a fluke pattern anymore, it's a trend line.

00:05:58.180 --> 00:06:19.340
<v Casey>Meanwhile the FDA is moving toward tighter restrictions on food ingredients. The New York Times reports a proposed rule would require companies to notify the agency before adding new ingredients to their products — closing a loophole critics have flagged for years. Whether the agency has the staffing to vet everything coming through that pipeline is a separate, open question.

00:06:20.658 --> 00:06:25.682
<v Riley>Passing a rule and funding the people who enforce it are two different budget lines.

00:06:26.882 --> 00:06:48.786
<v Riley>Different kind of disruption entirely — Fast Company has a piece on how Ukraine is hitting Russia's e-commerce infrastructure with sustained drone strikes. Wildberries, often described as Russia's answer to Amazon, has had warehouses and logistics hubs targeted for more than a month now, stretching from Moscow and St. Petersburg all the way to cities in the Ural Mountains.

00:06:49.907 --> 00:06:56.987
<v Casey>Supply chains as a battlefield isn't new, but a month of sustained strikes on retail logistics rather than military targets is striking.

00:06:58.186 --> 00:07:09.874
<v Casey>On the hardware side — MakeUseOf reports someone got a functioning language model running on an ESP32, an eight-dollar microcontroller chip. Twenty-eight point nine million parameters, no server required.

00:07:09.774 --> 00:07:22.862
<v Riley>That's absurd in a good way. That's the kind of quiet compounding progress that eventually shows up embedded in farm equipment sensors, running locally instead of needing a data connection out in the middle of a hay field.

00:07:24.062 --> 00:07:38.590
<v Riley>Mother Jones has a longer feature asking whether data centers could actually cost Republicans votes in Florida — residents in rural Citrus County pushing back on new data center builds eating into local water and power capacity near their homes.

00:07:39.838 --> 00:07:46.742
<v Casey>A fight playing out in a lot of small towns that thought they were getting an economic boost and got a utility strain instead.

00:07:47.942 --> 00:08:02.118
<v Casey>The New Yorker's running a longer feature on what they're calling "the great delegalization" — more than a million immigrants in the US seeing legal protections withdrawn in a short span. Heavier story, worth reading in full rather than summarizing here.

00:08:03.318 --> 00:08:16.846
<v Riley>TechCrunch reports a company called Discovered Materials raised nine million dollars hunting for new materials that keep computer chips cooler — the AI infrastructure boom is generating its own side industry just in thermal management.

00:08:18.046 --> 00:08:33.134
<v Casey>And ScienceAlert closes the theme block with something genuinely unexpected — researchers found that a common cholesterol drug might also pull PFAS and microplastics out of the bloodstream as a side effect. Not what it was designed to do, but a promising accidental discovery.

00:08:33.724 --> 00:08:38.164
<v Riley>File that one under science surprising even the scientists who ran the trial.

00:08:39.549 --> 00:08:47.701
<v Casey>And one bonus item before we move on — rare white magpies were spotted west of Williams Lake this week, described as roughly a one-in-a-million sighting.

00:08:49.035 --> 00:08:57.331
<v Riley>A nice reminder the Cariboo still produces news that has nothing to do with fire season. That's the roundup — let's get to something a little warmer.

00:08:58.731 --> 00:09:24.211
<v Casey>Today's spotlight is on the Cariboo Chilcotin Coast Tourism Association. They're the regional destination marketing organization covering everything from the Chilcotin plateau to the coast — helping small tourism operators, guest ranches, and outdoor recreation businesses get found by visitors who'd otherwise never know they exist. If you run a lodge, a trail system, or a farm-gate operation out here, their site at landwithoutlimits dot com is worth a look.

00:09:24.870 --> 00:09:39.302
<v Riley>Tourism and ranching overlap more than people think out here — a lot of working ranches host guests to diversify income when cattle prices swing, so an organization helping them get visibility is doing real economic work, not just marketing.

00:09:40.202 --> 00:09:42.986
<v Casey>Which is actually a decent bridge into today's Deep Dive.

00:09:44.386 --> 00:09:58.274
<v Riley>There's a question floating around tech circles this year that's worth putting straight to the people who actually work the land — why does an entire industry that was supposed to be building the future keep describing its own work as pointless?

00:09:58.549 --> 00:10:01.813
<v Casey>(wry) And nobody's writing a manifesto about the calling of cattle feeding.

00:10:02.427 --> 00:10:29.323
<v Riley>Right — because nobody assumes a rancher needs their job to feel meaningful in some abstract sense. The cattle need feeding whether or not you find it fulfilling that week. But there's a real story right now that puts these two worlds side by side. Watch Duty, the wildfire tracking app, is expanding into flood monitoring and building toward a full disaster-warning platform. Fast Company put their user base at ten million this year.

00:10:29.875 --> 00:10:34.203
<v Casey>Built by people who, by every account, believe they're doing something that matters.

00:10:34.736 --> 00:11:04.608
<v Riley>Which is the opposite of the malaise story. Watch Duty's CEO told Fast Company this fire season is the worst his team has faced — British Columbia's under a province-wide state of emergency, more than twenty thousand people evacuated, and Spokane just had the most destructive urban firestorm in Washington state history. Over seven hundred structures, more than ten thousand acres burned. And the tool built largely by volunteers is the one keeping pace with it in real time.

00:11:05.107 --> 00:11:22.195
<v Casey>Or the tool is filling a hole that used to be a government job. A South Korean outlet covering this story framed Watch Duty's growth explicitly against the backdrop of cuts to FEMA. That's not innovation outrunning bureaucracy — that's volunteer labor absorbing what public disaster response used to fund and staff.

00:11:22.857 --> 00:11:28.657
<v Riley>Both things can be true at once. The tool works, and it exists because something else stopped working.

00:11:29.223 --> 00:11:50.503
<v Casey>Then don't call it a tech success story without the asterisk. Call it a symptom with a good user interface. And here's the ranching angle — the Bald Range fire we covered a few minutes ago destroyed around a hundred and fifty structures in our own region. Ranchers there don't get a ten-million-user app deciding when to move cattle off summer range. They get a truck, a radio, and neighbours.

00:11:51.013 --> 00:12:09.357
<v Riley>That informal network we've talked about before — B.C. ranchers pooling equipment and sharing range intel by phone tree because formal channels move too slowly for the pace a fire moves at — is the opposite of Silicon Valley's instinct, which is always to build a platform first and ask who needs it second.

00:12:10.010 --> 00:12:25.746
<v Casey>(dry) Sounds like the world's oldest API, and it's worked longer than any platform has existed. Nobody at that phone tree describes their job as meaningless. They don't have the luxury of that particular crisis — the crisis in front of them is immediate and physical, not existential.

00:12:26.397 --> 00:12:52.805
<v Riley>That's the actual contrast. The malaise in tech doesn't come from the work being fake — a lot of it produces real tools, Watch Duty being a genuine example. It comes from the distance between the work and any tangible consequence. An engineer ships a feature and gets a dashboard metric back. A rancher moves cattle off a burning range and either the herd survives or it doesn't. There's no ambiguity in that feedback loop.

00:12:53.477 --> 00:13:12.373
<v Casey>Immediate consequence doesn't guarantee job satisfaction either, though. Ranching income has been squeezed for years — cattle prices, drought, and now fire risk stacked on top of all of it. Ask a producer whether physical stakes make the work feel meaningful or just exhausting, and depending on the year, you'll get very different answers.

00:13:12.962 --> 00:13:38.554
<v Riley>Exhausting and pointless aren't the same complaint, though. Tech's malaise, as it's being described, is specifically about purpose — people who signed up believing they were changing the world and now can't articulate what their work actually accomplishes. Nobody on a ranch loses sleep over whether raising cattle is a coherent mission. The mission was never in question. It's whether the mission is survivable financially.

00:13:39.053 --> 00:13:46.125
<v Casey>Okay — existential doubt versus economic doubt. Different disease, even if the symptoms both look like exhaustion from the outside.

00:13:46.789 --> 00:14:00.325
<v Riley>And it maps onto why Watch Duty's expansion is interesting rather than just cynical. The engineers building it aren't asking "does this matter" in the abstract — a burning town answers that question for them in real time, every single shift.

00:14:01.002 --> 00:14:25.898
<v Casey>It's also a fragile model to build public safety infrastructure around. Volunteer-run, nonprofit-funded, scaling from wildfire into flood tracking on top of everything else — what happens the year donations dip, or a bad actor spoofs an alert during an actual evacuation? Ranchers dealing with a phone tree know exactly who's accountable when information turns out to be wrong. Who's accountable when Watch Duty gets it wrong at ten million users?

00:14:26.189 --> 00:14:51.677
<v Riley>That's a governance gap, not evidence the tool shouldn't exist. Some mining operations already treat wildfire response as a permanent line item now — helicopter contracts, air quality monitoring, revised operating schedules built into annual budgets rather than treated as emergency spending. That's the model public disaster infrastructure should be following, whether or not a nonprofit app fills the gap in the interim.

00:14:52.352 --> 00:15:14.944
<v Casey>Which still leaves the interim gap being filled by people who signed up for a nonprofit mission, not a public mandate with public accountability attached. That's the same underlying complaint under a different name — mission-driven idealism carrying institutional weight it was never designed to carry, and eventually that idealism either burns out or gets bought out by somebody with different incentives.

00:15:15.444 --> 00:15:59.244
<v Riley>There's a second layer to this — the actual cause of the fires filling everyone's summer with smoke deserves scrutiny too. There's a real dispute buried under the standard climate framing. The Canadian Climate Institute frames this as a climate-driven crisis hitting the forest sector hard — over one and a half percent of the national economy tied up in forestry, with real trade disruption stacked on the ecological damage. But the CO2 Coalition, a group openly skeptical of mainstream climate framing, argues it's Canada's unmanaged, aging forests and permitting choices doing more of the work than climate change alone — and that the smoke reaching Detroit and New York is a policy failure just as much as a climate one.

00:15:59.862 --> 00:16:21.510
<v Casey>That tracks with something we got into a couple of weeks back on the aspen-spraying story — commercially valuable conifers replacing natural firebreaks because that's what pays the mills, even though it makes the whole forest burn faster and hotter. If that's even partly true, "climate change" is absorbing blame that belongs to land-management decisions made decades ago and never revisited.

00:16:22.174 --> 00:16:42.294
<v Riley>That's a genuinely uncomfortable point for the tech-optimist case in me, because no app fixes a forest that was planted wrong forty years ago. Watch Duty can tell you a fire is coming faster and give you more warning time. It can't tell you the fire wouldn't be burning nearly that hot if the stand composition on the land base were different.

00:16:42.967 --> 00:17:07.383
<v Casey>And ranchers on Crown range have been saying some version of this for years without anyone framing it as a live debate — that fuel load management got deprioritized, that forestry extension and advisory capacity got gutted a decade ago and never restored, and the fire seasons since then are the bill coming due on that decision. Nobody needed a NASA smoke map to notice that pattern. They watched it happen on their own range.

00:17:07.883 --> 00:17:33.451
<v Riley>Which brings this back to the original question in a way I didn't expect going in. The tech worker's despair is partly about not knowing if the work connects to anything real. The rancher's frustration is closer to the opposite problem — knowing exactly what's wrong, for years, and having no lever available to pull on the actual root cause. Neither one is short on meaning, in the end. Both are short on control over the outcome.

00:17:33.994 --> 00:17:51.570
<v Casey>That's the sharper framing, honestly. Tech's crisis is existential — a search for purpose. The land economy's crisis is structural — everyone here can name the fuel-load problem, the forestry policy failure, the extension-funding cuts, in detail. Naming it accurately for a decade hasn't changed who actually gets to act on it.

00:17:52.226 --> 00:18:11.482
<v Riley>So maybe the honest answer to why so much of tech feels sad is that a lot of knowledge workers got handed the meaning question and mistook it for the hardest problem available, when the harder problem — the one ranchers here have lived with for the better part of a decade — is having a clear diagnosis and zero authority to act on the fix.

00:18:12.382 --> 00:18:38.622
<v Casey>Which raises the actual structural question that follows from all this — who gets a seat at the table when the next wildfire-tech tool gets designed. Watch Duty was built by engineers and journalists, largely from outside the communities it now serves. If a similar platform ever gets built specifically for Cariboo ranchers, does it get shaped by product teams in California, or by the people who've read range behaviour and fire spread on this exact land for thirty years?

00:18:38.947 --> 00:19:09.035
<v Riley>That's a real design fork, and it's not hypothetical — the Tŝilhqot'in National Government and Nations like Xatśūll already run their own fire and land monitoring programs tied to territory-specific knowledge, not an imported dashboard dropped in from somewhere else. If a regional alert tool ever gets built for the Cariboo specifically, that's the governance model worth copying — local advisory input designed in before the first line of code gets written, not bolted on after a pilot quietly fails.

00:19:09.687 --> 00:19:24.759
<v Casey>That's a concrete ask, not a slogan — a producer association or a Nation-led monitoring program getting actual formal design input on any wildfire tech that claims to serve this region, instead of being handed a finished app from outside and told to adopt it and like it.

00:19:25.359 --> 00:19:40.143
<v Riley>And it's testable. Next fire season, ask whether any regional tool credits rancher or Nation input in its design decisions publicly, or whether it's still a top-down build imported from outside the Cariboo with no local fingerprints on it at all.

00:19:40.791 --> 00:19:54.751
<v Casey>If anyone listening has a read on how that ranching phone-tree system actually runs day to day, or knows of an attempt to build a local alert network from the ground up rather than importing one, send it our way — feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a.

00:19:55.270 --> 00:20:04.326
<v Riley>We'd want to hear that. On a season like this, the tools matter less than who's steering them — and that's a fight to push on before next fire season, not after.

00:20:04.971 --> 00:20:10.843
<v Casey>(deadpan) Until ranchers and Nations get an actual seat in that design process, I'm not holding my breath. See you tomorrow.

00:20:11.203 --> 00:20:12.715
<v Riley>(warmly) Stay safe out there, everyone.